u may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Good, I can see the board is back to the original state. Let me focus on just the key objects:\n- Purple objects (p): 3 at rows 7-10, cols 14-16, 20-22, 26-28\n- Green blocks (N): need to find them\n- Sky blue (S): background areas\n- Player (b): somewhere\nLet me try a more targeted approach. The key insight from the first successful run is that I need to click green blocks in a specific order that triggers the board to scroll at the right time, revealing new green blocks below.\nLet me try: click 8 green blocks to trigger the scroll, then click the NEW green blocks that appear below (at rows 39, 45, etc.) instead of the remaining original green blocks.\nBut first, let me understand the board layout better. The green blocks are at rows 25-35 (2 rows of 7 blocks each). After clicking 8, the board scrolls and reveals new green blocks at rows 39, 45, etc.\nLet me try clicking 8 green blocks first, then check what new green blocks appear.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Focus on key objects only\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nnodes = seg['nodes']\n\n# Find 